Object-Oriented Programming (OOP) is a powerful paradigm that forms the foundation of Java development. Let's explore the key concepts and see them in action!

## Core OOP Concepts in Java

### 1. Classes and Objects 📦

Classes are blueprints for objects, while objects are instances of classes.

```java
// Class definition
public class Car {
    // Fields (attributes)
    private String model;
    private String color;
    private int year;

    // Constructor
    public Car(String model, String color, int year) {
        this.model = model;
        this.color = color;
        this.year = year;
    }

    // Method
    public void startEngine() {
        System.out.println(model + " engine started!");
    }
}

// Creating objects
Car myCar = new Car("Toyota", "Blue", 2022);
myCar.startEngine(); // Output: Toyota engine started!
```

### 2. Encapsulation 🔒

Encapsulation is hiding internal state and requiring all interaction to be performed through an object's methods.

```java
public class BankAccount {
    // Private fields - hidden from outside
    private double balance;
    private String accountNumber;

    // Public methods - controlled access
    public void deposit(double amount) {
        if (amount > 0) {
            balance += amount;
            System.out.println("Deposited: $" + amount);
        }
    }

    public double getBalance() {
        return balance;
    }
}
```

### 3. Inheritance 👨‍👦

Inheritance allows a class to inherit attributes and methods from another class.

```java
// Parent class
public class Animal {
    protected String name;

    public Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void eat() {
        System.out.println(name + " is eating");
    }
}

// Child class
public class Dog extends Animal {
    private String breed;

    public Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name); // Call parent constructor
        this.breed = breed;
    }

    public void bark() {
        System.out.println(name + " is barking!");
    }
}

// Usage
Dog myDog = new Dog("Rex", "German Shepherd");
myDog.eat();  // Inherited method
myDog.bark(); // Dog-specific method
```

### 4. Polymorphism 🔄

Polymorphism allows objects to take on many forms. In Java, this happens through method overriding and overloading.

```java
// Method overriding example
public class Shape {
    public double calculateArea() {
        return 0;
    }
}

public class Circle extends Shape {
    private double radius;

    public Circle(double radius) {
        this.radius = radius;
    }

    @Override
    public double calculateArea() {
        return Math.PI * radius * radius;
    }
}

public class Rectangle extends Shape {
    private double width;
    private double height;

    public Rectangle(double width, double height) {
        this.width = width;
        this.height = height;
    }

    @Override
    public double calculateArea() {
        return width * height;
    }
}

// Polymorphic behavior
Shape myCircle = new Circle(5);
Shape myRectangle = new Rectangle(4, 6);

System.out.println(myCircle.calculateArea());    // Uses Circle's implementation
System.out.println(myRectangle.calculateArea()); // Uses Rectangle's implementation
```

### 5. Abstraction 🧩

Abstraction focuses on what an object does instead of how it does it.

```java
// Abstract class
public abstract class Vehicle {
    private String registrationNumber;

    public Vehicle(String registrationNumber) {
        this.registrationNumber = registrationNumber;
    }

    // Abstract method - must be implemented by subclasses
    public abstract void move();

    // Concrete method
    public String getRegistrationNumber() {
        return registrationNumber;
    }
}

// Concrete implementation
public class Car extends Vehicle {
    public Car(String registrationNumber) {
        super(registrationNumber);
    }

    @Override
    public void move() {
        System.out.println("Car drives on the road");
    }
}
```

### 6. Interfaces 🔌

Interfaces define a contract that implementing classes must follow.

```java
public interface Playable {
    void play();
    void stop();
}

public class MusicPlayer implements Playable {
    @Override
    public void play() {
        System.out.println("Music playing...");
    }

    @Override
    public void stop() {
        System.out.println("Music stopped");
    }
}
```

## Putting It All Together: A Complete Example

Let's create a simple library management system to demonstrate these concepts working together:

```java
// Abstract base class
abstract class LibraryItem {
    private String title;
    private String itemID;
    private boolean checkedOut;

    public LibraryItem(String title, String itemID) {
        this.title = title;
        this.itemID = itemID;
        this.checkedOut = false;
    }

    // Abstract method
    public abstract void displayInfo();

    // Concrete methods
    public void checkOut() {
        if (!checkedOut) {
            checkedOut = true;
            System.out.println(title + " has been checked out");
        } else {
            System.out.println(title + " is already checked out");
        }
    }

    public void returnItem() {
        if (checkedOut) {
            checkedOut = false;
            System.out.println(title + " has been returned");
        } else {
            System.out.println(title + " was not checked out");
        }
    }

    // Getters
    public String getTitle() { return title; }
    public String getItemID() { return itemID; }
    public boolean isCheckedOut() { return checkedOut; }
}

// Interface
interface Borrowable {
    int getLoanPeriod();
    boolean isOverdue();
}

// Concrete class extending abstract class and implementing interface
class Book extends LibraryItem implements Borrowable {
    private String author;
    private int pages;
    private String genre;

    public Book(String title, String itemID, String author, int pages, String genre) {
        super(title, itemID);
        this.author = author;
        this.pages = pages;
        this.genre = genre;
    }

    @Override
    public void displayInfo() {
        System.out.println("Book: " + getTitle());
        System.out.println("ID: " + getItemID());
        System.out.println("Author: " + author);
        System.out.println("Pages: " + pages);
        System.out.println("Genre: " + genre);
        System.out.println("Status: " + (isCheckedOut() ? "Checked Out" : "Available"));
    }

    @Override
    public int getLoanPeriod() {
        return 21; // 21 days loan period
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isOverdue() {
        // In a real application, would compare current date with due date
        return false;
    }
}

// Another concrete class showing inheritance
class DVD extends LibraryItem implements Borrowable {
    private int duration; // in minutes
    private String director;

    public DVD(String title, String itemID, int duration, String director) {
        super(title, itemID);
        this.duration = duration;
        this.director = director;
    }

    @Override
    public void displayInfo() {
        System.out.println("DVD: " + getTitle());
        System.out.println("ID: " + getItemID());
        System.out.println("Director: " + director);
        System.out.println("Duration: " + duration + " minutes");
        System.out.println("Status: " + (isCheckedOut() ? "Checked Out" : "Available"));
    }

    @Override
    public int getLoanPeriod() {
        return 7; // 7 days loan period
    }

    @Override
    public boolean isOverdue() {
        // In a real application, would compare current date with due date
        return false;
    }
}

// Usage example
public class LibrarySystem {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Creating objects
        Book book = new Book("Java Programming", "B001", "John Doe", 450, "Technical");
        DVD dvd = new DVD("Inception", "D001", 148, "Christopher Nolan");

        // Using polymorphism
        LibraryItem[] items = {book, dvd};

        for (LibraryItem item : items) {
            item.displayInfo();
            item.checkOut();

            // Using instanceof to work with specific types
            if (item instanceof Borrowable) {
                Borrowable borrowable = (Borrowable) item;
                System.out.println("Loan period: " + borrowable.getLoanPeriod() + " days");
            }

            System.out.println("-------------------");
        }
    }
}
```

## Key OOP Principles to Remember

1. **Encapsulate what varies** - Hide implementation details behind interfaces
2. **Favor composition over inheritance** - Build flexible systems by composing objects
3. **Program to interfaces, not implementations** - Depend on abstractions
4. **Strive for loosely coupled designs** - Minimize dependencies between components
5. **Classes should be open for extension but closed for modification** - The Open/Closed Principle
6. **DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself)** - Eliminate duplication by abstracting common behavior

## Next Steps to Master OOP in Java

1. Practice creating class hierarchies for different domains
2. Implement common design patterns (Singleton, Factory, Observer, etc.)
3. Learn about SOLID principles for better OOP design
4. Explore Java's built-in OOP features like generics and lambda expressions
5. Study real-world Java codebases to see OOP in action

